# Marquise Diamond Clarity Guide {{year}}

## TL;DR: 5 Spots That Signal Marquise Clarity Rejection

**Marquise diamond clarity guide** rules are location-driven, not just grade-driven. The GIA clarity grade (FL, IF, VVS1, VVS2, VS1, VS2, SI1, SI2) tells you how many inclusions exist. The marquise shape dictates where those inclusions matter. Here are the 5 inclusion zones that force a walk-away regardless of overall grade:

- **Spot 1 — Either pointed tip (within 2mm of the point).** Any inclusion here creates a structural fracture risk when the ring takes impact.
- **Spot 2 — The girdle edge.** Inclusions at the girdle chip during normal wear and the stone loses weight.
- **Spot 3 — Directly below the table (face-up view).** The most visible zone when looking at the stone — any crystal here shows through.
- **Spot 4 — The pavilion facets below the belly.** These reflect inclusions back up through the crown — a hidden inclusion here still shows.
- **Spot 5 — Clustered inclusions anywhere in the center zone.** Multiple small inclusions concentrated together create a visible shadow even at VS1 clarity.

→ Our pick: filter for VS2 clarity with inclusions outside these 5 zones → [see live 1 ct marquise VS2 inventory on Blue Nile](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29764389?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

**Priority:** VS2 is the clarity floor for marquise. Clarity grades above VS2 (VS1, VVS2, VVS1, IF, FL) are only worth buying if you can verify the specific stone has no inclusions in the 5 rejection zones. A VS2 marquise with inclusions in safe zones outperforms a VVS1 marquise with inclusions in the tip zones.

## Expert Summary

Marquise diamond clarity floor is VS2 — the same floor as round brilliant diamonds. The twist is that marquise clarity depends more on inclusion location than on the GIA grade itself. The 56-facet brilliant-cut pattern of marquise masks small inclusions in the main pavilion zones but highlights inclusions near the pointed tips, at the girdle, directly below the table, and in the centerline where bow-tie shadow forms. Real Blue Nile inventory audited {{month_year}} shows 1 ct natural marquise prices rising from VS2 at $4,049 (I color) to VVS1 at $7,681 (D color) to IF/FL premium tiers at $7,000+ — a 90% premium for clarity grades the eye cannot see. Smart buyers stay at VS2 and verify the GIA plot shows all inclusions in safe zones. This guide walks the 5 rejection zones, the GIA plot verification method, the bow-tie masking bonus, and real Blue Nile pricing across every clarity tier. For a shape-matched proportion framework see our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) and the [marquise diamond color guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide) for the color-grade pairing.

## The GIA Clarity Scale Applied to Marquise

GIA grades clarity on an 11-step scale from FL (Flawless) at the top to I3 (Included) at the bottom. The marquise-relevant tiers are:

- **FL (Flawless):** No inclusions visible under 10× magnification. Rarest tier. Premium pricing.
- **IF (Internally Flawless):** No internal inclusions; minor surface blemishes OK. Very rare.
- **VVS1 (Very Very Slightly Included 1):** Inclusions extremely difficult to see at 10× magnification. Microscope-only.
- **VVS2 (Very Very Slightly Included 2):** Inclusions very difficult to see at 10×. Microscope-only.
- **VS1 (Very Slightly Included 1):** Inclusions difficult to see at 10×. Eye-clean guaranteed.
- **VS2 (Very Slightly Included 2):** Inclusions somewhat easy to see at 10×. Eye-clean 85-95% at 1 ct.
- **SI1 (Slightly Included 1):** Inclusions easy to see at 10×. Eye-clean 50-70% at 1 ct, drops at larger sizes.
- **SI2 (Slightly Included 2):** Inclusions very easy to see at 10×. Risky even at 1 ct, fails above 1.5 ct.
- **I1-I3 (Included):** Inclusions visible to naked eye. Never for engagement rings.

For marquise diamond clarity guide purposes, the usable range is VS2 at the floor to VVS2 at the ceiling. FL/IF/VVS1 are overpricing — nobody can see the difference between VS2 and VVS1 without a microscope. SI1 can work if the specific stone passes the 5-zone test, but requires more verification work.

Our [diamond clarity chart](/diamond-clarity-chart) covers the full 11-tier scale with inclusion-type photo references. For marquise specifically, the location-based rules override the raw grade — a VS2 stone with inclusions in safe zones beats a VVS1 stone with inclusions in the rejection zones.

## The 5-Zone Rejection Map — Where Marquise Inclusions Matter Most

Here is the first concept — **The 5-Zone Rejection Map**. These are the specific areas inside a marquise where any inclusion means walk away, regardless of overall grade.

### Zone 1 — The Pointed Tips (Both Ends)

The two pointed tips at either end of a marquise are the most structurally vulnerable areas of the stone. Each tip tapers from the belly down to a point, which means the material thins dramatically toward the tip.

If an inclusion sits within 2mm of either tip, three bad things happen:

1. **Fracture risk.** Impact to the ring during daily wear concentrates stress at the tip. An inclusion in this zone creates a weak point that can propagate into a chip or a crack.
2. **Visibility amplification.** Light passing through the thin tip material lights up any inclusion in the zone like a flashlight through tissue paper.
3. **Resale penalty.** Tip inclusions reduce resale value 20-30% beyond the standard clarity-grade discount.

GIA plots show inclusion locations as symbols on the diamond outline diagram. For marquise, draw an imaginary line 2mm in from each tip — any inclusion symbol inside those zones means reject.

### Zone 2 — The Girdle Edge

The girdle is the outermost edge of the diamond — the thin band around the perimeter. Inclusions touching or crossing the girdle create two problems: they weaken the stone's structural integrity at the setting contact point, and they are directly exposed to prong wear during daily use.

Girdle inclusions include "feathers reaching to girdle" (fracture lines that run to the edge) and "crystals at girdle." Any GIA plot showing an inclusion symbol touching the perimeter outline is a reject.

Our [round diamond inclusions types](/round-diamond-inclusions-types) guide covers the full inclusion-type taxonomy applied to round brilliants. The same inclusion types matter in marquise, with the extra constraint that girdle inclusions near the pointed tips compound both risks.

### Zone 3 — Directly Below the Table (Face-Up View)

The table is the flat facet at the top of the diamond — the biggest facet, directly in your line of sight when you look at the ring. Inclusions directly beneath the table are the most visible inclusions in the stone, period.

GIA plot verification: locate the table outline on the diagram (the roughly oval shape in the center of the face-up diagram). Any inclusion symbol inside that outline shows up in the stone's face-up view. For VS1 or VS2 clarity, "shows up" means visible with careful magnification only. For SI1, it means potentially visible to the naked eye.

If you are buying SI1 and want eye-clean, the table zone must be clear. VS2 and above tolerate small inclusions here if they are needle-type or pinpoint. Clouds and crystals in the table zone are rejects regardless of grade.

### Zone 4 — The Pavilion Facets Below the Belly

Light enters the diamond through the crown, reflects off the pavilion facets, and returns to your eye through the crown. The pavilion facets just below the widest point of the stone (the belly) do the most reflection work. Any inclusion on these facets gets amplified by the reflection and shows back through the crown.

This is the "hidden inclusion" risk. An inclusion deep in the pavilion that you cannot see on the top of the GIA plot can still show through the top of the stone because of pavilion reflection. The GIA plot shows pavilion inclusions as symbols on the pavilion-view diagram (the bottom diagram). Any crystal, cloud, or feather symbol in the belly-adjacent pavilion zone is a reject.

### Zone 5 — Clustered Inclusions in the Center Zone

Even if no single inclusion is severe, multiple small inclusions clustered together can create a visible shadow or cloudy area. The center zone — roughly the middle 50% of the stone face-up — is where clustered inclusions matter most.

GIA plots show clustered inclusions as groups of symbols close together. If the plot shows 4+ inclusion symbols within the center zone, even at VS1 or VS2 clarity, the stone can look cloudy or "milky" in natural light. Walk away from clustered-center stones regardless of grade.

> **Farzana's Verdict:** The 5-Zone Rejection Map is the single most useful filter I use for marquise clarity. Buyers who skip this and only look at the grade label buy stones with VVS1 certificates that look worse than my VS2 picks. The GIA plot takes 60 seconds to read once you know the zones. Do the check. Mark the plot. If anything is in a rejection zone, move to the next stone. Blue Nile has enough inventory that you never have to settle on a bad-location stone.

## The Bow-Tie Mask Bonus — When Marquise Inclusions Hide

Here is the second concept — **The Bow-Tie Mask Bonus**. The dark bow-tie zone in the center of marquise diamonds (see our [marquise diamond bow tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie)) is a bug for appearance but a feature for clarity.

Any inclusion that sits in the exact bow-tie shadow zone is partially or fully masked because the area is already dark. A pinpoint or crystal inclusion that would be visible at 10× magnification in a bright zone can be invisible in the bow-tie shadow.

This is the one case where a mild bow-tie (Severity 2) actually benefits the buyer. If your marquise has a faint bow-tie AND an inclusion in the belly centerline, the two problems cancel out — the shadow hides the inclusion.

The practical application: when verifying clarity on a stone with faint bow-tie, cross-check the GIA plot with the HD video. If the plot shows an inclusion in the center but the video shows nothing visible in the bow-tie zone, the inclusion is masked and the stone is a safe buy at a lower clarity grade.

**Important limit:** The Bow-Tie Mask Bonus only applies to faint (Severity 1-2) bow-ties. Heavy bow-tie (Severity 4-5) is still a reject regardless of what it hides. See the [marquise diamond bow tie severity scale](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie) for the full 1-5 framework.

## VS2 Is the Marquise Clarity Floor — Here Is Why

VS2 is the lowest clarity grade that is reliably eye-clean at 1 ct marquise. "Eye-clean" means no inclusions visible to the naked eye at normal viewing distance (10-12 inches from the stone).

Real-world eye-clean rate by clarity grade at 1 ct marquise:
- **VVS1, VVS2:** 100% eye-clean. Microscope-only inclusions. Overpaying.
- **VS1:** 100% eye-clean. Still microscope-only inclusions.
- **VS2:** 85-95% eye-clean. Inclusions visible with careful 10× but not naked-eye. Sweet spot.
- **SI1:** 50-70% eye-clean. Half the stones at this grade have naked-eye visible inclusions.
- **SI2:** 20-30% eye-clean. Most stones at this grade have visible inclusions.

The VS2-vs-VS1 pricing gap at 1 ct natural is small — around $400-800 depending on color. The VS2-vs-SI1 gap is also small but the risk difference is big. VS2 is the right spot: the price saves vs higher clarity grades and the eye-clean guarantee is high.

Our [round diamond vvs vs vs2](/round-diamond-vvs-vs-vs2) and [round diamond vs1 vs vs2](/round-diamond-vs1-vs-vs2) guides cover the same clarity-tier logic applied to round brilliants.

