## Clarity Grade Decision by Lighting Scenario

Clarity visibility depends partly on lighting. Different lighting conditions change what inclusions appear acceptable.

**Daylight / outdoor sun.** The hardest lighting for your clarity check — direct bright light illuminates every inclusion. VS2 inclusions can be visible with careful inspection. SI1 inclusions usually visible. You should pick your clarity assuming she will wear the ring outside most days.

**Jewelry store LED lighting.** The lighting store jewelers use is designed to maximize sparkle and mask inclusions. Any stone you look at here looks good. Do not use store lighting as your clarity judge.

**Office fluorescent lighting.** The hardest lighting for marquise appearance generally — bow-tie amplifies, color concentrates, inclusions show. VS2 holds up but SI1 can look worse than it did in the store you bought it from.

**Candlelit / dim restaurant.** Easiest lighting for clarity. All inclusions mask. Even SI2 stones look fine. But she will not spend her life in candlelight.

Your decision rule: pick clarity for the harshest lighting she actually wears the ring in. For most buyers, that is daytime outdoor light. VS2 is reliable at daylight for 1 ct. VS1 is reliable at daylight for 2 ct. Above those sizes, your slight visibility risk at VS2 climbs.

Our [round diamond eye clean guide](/round-diamond-eye-clean-guide) covers the full eye-clean verification method with lighting-condition examples. The same principles apply to marquise.

## Clarity Interaction With Proportions and Color

Clarity does not stand alone. The decision interacts with the stone's other quality factors.

**Clarity + color.** Lower color grades (H, I) mask minor clarity flaws because the slight warmth of the diamond partially camouflages pinpoint and needle inclusions. Buying an H-VS2 stone is less risky than buying a D-VS2 stone with the same inclusion plot, because the D color shows every spot more clearly. See our [marquise diamond color guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide) for the color-grade framework.

**Clarity + proportions.** Correct proportions (depth 59-62%, pavilion angle 40.6-41.8°) return more light to the eye, which reflects back any inclusions in the pavilion zone. A poorly proportioned marquise hides pavilion inclusions better simply because less light bounces back through them. This is one case where bad proportions actually "help" clarity, but you lose more in sparkle than you gain in clarity masking. Prioritize proportions. See our [marquise cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

**Clarity + L:W ratio.** More elongated L:W (2.00+) creates thinner tips with less material depth. Inclusions near the tips show through more at elongated ratios. If your stone is L:W 2.05+, bump clarity up one grade from the VS2 floor to VS1 to compensate for tip-zone visibility. See [marquise diamond length to width ratio](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

**Clarity + bow-tie.** The Bow-Tie Mask Bonus (above) applies to faint bow-ties only. Heavy bow-ties show inclusions worse because the eye is already drawn to the dark central area and any inclusion nearby pops out. Pick clarity after assessing bow-tie severity. See [marquise diamond bow tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

The compound rule: in the worst case (elongated L:W + heavy bow-tie + D color), bump to VVS2 clarity minimum. In the best case (classic L:W + no bow-tie + H color in warm metal), VS2 is fully safe.

