## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the minimum clarity for a marquise diamond?

VS2 is the minimum clarity for marquise diamonds. At VS2, 85-95% of 1 ct marquise stones are eye-clean (no inclusions visible to the naked eye). Below VS2 — at SI1 or SI2 — the eye-clean rate drops to 50-70% and 20-30% respectively. Stay at VS2 or above unless you can verify the specific SI1 stone passes the 5-zone test and shows eye-clean on HD video.

### Is VS1 worth it over VS2 for a marquise?

Usually no. The VS1-vs-VS2 pricing gap at 1 ct natural is only $100-$500 depending on color, and the visual difference is zero — both grades are eye-clean under normal viewing. Spend the $100-$500 on a higher color grade or better setting instead. The exception is 2+ ct marquise where VS2 inclusions can become slightly visible under harsh lighting — bump to VS1 at those sizes.

### Can I buy SI1 clarity for a marquise?

Yes, but with verification. SI1 marquises have a 50-70% eye-clean rate at 1 ct and the rate drops to 30-50% at 2 ct. If you can verify the specific stone passes the 5-zone test and shows no visible inclusions on HD video, SI1 can save $500-$1,500 vs VS2 at 1 ct. Never buy SI1 blind — always verify the specific stone.

### What are the 5 inclusion zones to avoid in marquise?

The 5 rejection zones are: (1) within 2mm of either pointed tip, (2) touching the girdle edge, (3) directly below the table in the face-up view, (4) on pavilion facets below the belly where reflection amplifies, (5) clustered inclusions in the center zone. Any inclusion in any of these 5 zones means walk away regardless of overall GIA grade.

### Does bow-tie hide inclusions in a marquise?

Yes, for faint bow-ties only. The dark central zone of a Severity 1-2 bow-tie masks pinpoint and crystal inclusions in the belly centerline because the shadow is already there. This is called The Bow-Tie Mask Bonus. Heavy bow-tie (Severity 4-5) does the opposite — the eye is drawn to the central zone and any inclusion pops out.

### How do I read a GIA plot for marquise clarity?

Pull up the Clarity Characteristics section of the GIA report. It shows two diamond-outline diagrams (crown view on top, pavilion view on bottom) with symbols marking inclusion types and locations. For marquise, draw mental lines 2mm in from each pointed tip and around the girdle perimeter. Any symbol inside those lines is a rejection zone. Also check for 4+ clustered symbols in the center area.

### What clarity should I pick for a 2 carat marquise?

VS2 clarity is still the right pick at 2 ct. The real H-VS2 2 ct marquise on Blue Nile at $22,421 is the single best-value 2 ct natural marquise available. Moving to VS1 adds $2,000-$5,000 for invisible-to-the-eye clarity improvement. Only move above VS2 if the specific VS2 stones fail the 5-zone test — not just because 2 ct "deserves" higher clarity.

### Is lab-grown marquise usually higher clarity than natural?

Yes. Lab-grown marquise inventory concentrates in VVS2, VVS1, IF, and FL clarity because the lab growing process produces fewer inclusions than natural formation. At the lab price tier, premium clarity is cheap — a 1 ct D-VVS2 lab marquise costs $657, which is less than natural VS2 at the same color. There is no clarity-saving math needed for lab buyers.

### What inclusions are safe for marquise clarity?

Pinpoint inclusions (tiny white dots), small needle inclusions (thin lines), and small internal graining are all safe for marquise at VS2 clarity if they sit outside the 5 rejection zones. Unsafe inclusion types include feathers (fracture lines), clouds (groups of pinpoints), crystals (visible mineral deposits), and any inclusion that touches the girdle or crosses facet junctions.

### Should I pay for VVS1 or IF/FL clarity on a marquise?

Almost never. VVS1 inclusions require 10× magnification to see — nobody looks at a diamond under a loupe during normal wear. IF and FL are even rarer and more expensive, with no visible improvement over VVS1. The $5,000+ premium between VS2 and IF/FL at 2 ct is pure certificate flex, not visible appearance. Collectors and investors buy these grades for the GIA report, not the stone.

### Does clarity grade affect marquise resale value?

Yes but less than carat and color. VS2 marquise resells at 40-50% of retail. VVS2 at 42-52%. VVS1 at 45-55%. The 5-point resale lift from grade jumps is small compared to the retail premium — buying VVS1 instead of VS2 costs you $600+ at purchase and recovers maybe $300 at resale. Not worth the carry cost.

### Can I see marquise clarity issues on Blue Nile HD video?

Partially. Blue Nile's HD video rotates each stone and lets you zoom to maximum magnification. Face-up visible inclusions (table zone, obvious crystals) show clearly on video. Pavilion-side inclusions and tip-zone inclusions can be harder to see from the standard viewing angle. Combine the video with the GIA plot to verify both face-up appearance and inclusion location.

### What is the eye-clean guarantee for VS2 marquise?

VS2 marquise diamonds are eye-clean 85-95% of the time at 1 ct, 80-90% at 1.5 ct, and 75-85% at 2 ct. The eye-clean rate drops as carat weight increases because larger stones have more facet surface area where inclusions can show. Always verify the specific VS2 stone on HD video rather than trusting the eye-clean rate alone.

### How does marquise clarity compare to round brilliant clarity?

Marquise clarity rules are stricter in the tip and girdle zones but similar in the pavilion and table zones. Round brilliants distribute inclusion visibility more evenly across the full face-up area, so a round brilliant can tolerate inclusions in places where a marquise cannot. In practice, use the same clarity floor (VS2) for both shapes but apply the 5-zone rejection map specifically to marquise.

### What is clarity enhancement and does it apply to marquise?

Clarity enhancement treatments include laser drilling (to reach and clear internal crystals) and fracture filling (to fill feather-type inclusions with glass-like material). These treatments can appear in marquise inventory but should be avoided — they reduce resale value by 50%+ and can degrade over time. GIA does not grade clarity-enhanced diamonds, so any stone that is enhanced will have a different lab report. Only buy GIA-graded marquise and the enhancement risk is zero.

